<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 803–<a href="/years/814">814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Krum the Fearsome, Khan Krum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krum was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> from about 803 to 814. He destroyed the remnants of the Avar Khaganate in the north-west and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> in 809. In 811 he trapped and killed the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a>, reportedly turning his skull into a drinking cup, and in 813 he defeated Michael I and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He is also credited with the first written Bulgarian laws.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krum.jpg" alt="Krum" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manasias · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krum33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Krum was born around the middle of the 8th century, but his family background and the circumstances of his accession are unknown. It has been speculated that he descended from Khan Kubrat through Kubrat's son Kuber. His name is probably of Turkic origin and may reflect a shortened form of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> word for rule, leadership or authority. He became khan sometime between 796 and 803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Around 805 Krum defeated the Avar Khaganate and advanced north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> region, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> a common border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a>. Bulgarian forces defeated a Byzantine army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Struma</a> valley in 807. In 809 he forced the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> and, despite a guarantee of safe conduct, slaughtered its garrison of 6,000. In 811 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a> advanced into Bulgaria, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> on 20 July and burned it. Krum armed as many men as he could, women and peasants included, and on 26 July trapped the retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in the Varbitsa Pass. The army was wiped out and Nikephoros killed. It is said that Krum had the emperor's skull lined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and used it as a drinking cup.</p><p>In 812 Krum took Develt and Mesembria. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-versinikia">Battle of Versinikia</a> on 22 June 813 he routed the army of Michael I Rangabe, and then advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he performed a pagan ceremony before the walls, sacrificing people and animals. He ordered a moat and rampart dug from Blachernae to the Golden Gate. Michael was forced to abdicate, the third emperor to lose his throne to Krum in as many years. Leo V the Armenian then tried to ambush Krum during talks; Krum escaped wounded, and in revenge burned churches and monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a> and took Adrianople.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Krum was preparing a great attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>, when he died on 13 April 814, most likely of a hemorrhage and stroke. During his reign Bulgarian territory doubled in size, reaching from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. He is remembered for the first known written Bulgarian law code, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to beggars and state protection to the poor and severely punished drinking, slander and robbery. Through these laws he became known as a strict but just ruler who brought Slavs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> into a centralised state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Krum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The eastern Avar lands were probably taken by the First Bulgarian Empire under <b>Khan Krum</b> early in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After killing the emperor Nikephoros I in 811, <b>Khan Krum</b> had the emperor's skull lined with silver and used it as a drinking cup, according to Byzantine sources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Byzantines had burned the Bulgar capital Pliska, <b>Khan Krum</b>'s forces trapped their army in a pass of the Balkan Mountains and destroyed almost all of it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Khan Krum</b> of Bulgaria routed the Byzantine army of Emperor Michael I Rangabe near Versinikia, not far from Adrianople in Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,27.13&amp;year=808" aria-label="Pliska on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5203 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Krum"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5653 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5653 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,27.13&amp;year=808">What was here in 808 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
